WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:03.002
-You mentioned Joe Kelly.
You mentioned Brendan Hunt.

00:00:03.002 --> 00:00:04.804
What those two guys have
in common with you and I is

00:00:04.804 --> 00:00:06.572
we all went over
and worked at this theater

00:00:06.572 --> 00:00:08.774
Boom Chicago in Amsterdam.

00:00:08.774 --> 00:00:11.977
I'm pretty sure that's where
all four of us learned to

00:00:11.977 --> 00:00:14.580
fall in love with soccer,
none more, it should be noted,

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:19.085
than Brendan Hunt, who plays
your assistant coach, Beard.

00:00:19.085 --> 00:00:21.287
But what was
your relationship --

00:00:21.287 --> 00:00:23.289
Because you came over
a couple years after me.

00:00:23.289 --> 00:00:25.491
I went in '98.
There was a World Cup going on.

00:00:25.491 --> 00:00:26.793
Holland did really well.
-Yeah.

00:00:26.793 --> 00:00:28.294
-The city came alive,
and I was like,

00:00:28.294 --> 00:00:29.595
"Oh, I want to be
a part of this forever."

00:00:29.595 --> 00:00:31.430
What was your in?

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:35.868
-It was initially playing
PlayStation, you know,

00:00:35.868 --> 00:00:37.403
in the green room,
in the shiny there,

00:00:37.403 --> 00:00:41.040
that I cajoled everybody to chip
in 20 gilders, before the Euro,

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:43.209
so that we could get
a PlayStation,

00:00:43.209 --> 00:00:45.378
mostly so I could play "FIFA"
with Brendan

00:00:45.378 --> 00:00:48.280
and "Tony Hawk Pro Skater"
alone.

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:52.318
And it was 2000,
so it was like, you know,

00:00:52.318 --> 00:00:54.987
the main teams were
Arsenal and Man U.

00:00:54.987 --> 00:00:56.989
Brendan, being a huge Arsenal
fan, would take them,

00:00:56.989 --> 00:00:59.426
so it was just battles of,
like, Henry versus Beckham.

00:00:59.426 --> 00:01:02.695
And Brendan is,
much like his character,

00:01:02.695 --> 00:01:06.298
a fountain of knowledge
and enthusiasm and ability

00:01:06.298 --> 00:01:08.167
to speak in parallels
of metaphor,

00:01:08.167 --> 00:01:10.036
and he would explain the game
to me as we were going.

00:01:10.036 --> 00:01:12.405
So then, I started to map,
like -- because he would explain

00:01:12.405 --> 00:01:15.140
using the '90s Bulls
and the triangle offense,

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:17.376
and I started to understand
the tactics of the game

00:01:17.376 --> 00:01:19.078
a little bit better and
the backstory of the players

00:01:19.078 --> 00:01:21.881
and how well they translate
that into the "FIFA" game.

00:01:21.881 --> 00:01:24.751
And then by the time we did --
once the commercials came up

00:01:24.751 --> 00:01:25.785
and the opportunity, I was like,

00:01:25.785 --> 00:01:27.554
"Oh, I know exactly
who to hire."

00:01:27.554 --> 00:01:30.823
Like, you know, or who to bring
on, and that's Brendan and Joe.

00:01:30.823 --> 00:01:34.594
And then, we would add, like,
a little in the contract, like,

00:01:34.594 --> 00:01:36.996
"Hey, we also want to be able to
come over, you know,

00:01:36.996 --> 00:01:39.399
and be given, like,
the red carpet treatment."

00:01:39.399 --> 00:01:43.569
You know, mostly for Brendan
to go see a game at Emirates,

00:01:43.569 --> 00:01:45.805
which is such a joy
to get to do.

00:01:45.805 --> 00:01:47.640
I know you feel the same way,

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:49.809
taking, like, your pops to go
see a Steelers game and stuff.

00:01:49.809 --> 00:01:53.780
When you get to have
these people treat you so well

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:55.914
and your friends and family.

00:01:55.914 --> 00:01:59.385
-This has completely upped
your exposure level

00:01:59.385 --> 00:02:01.787
to international athletes,
correct?

00:02:01.787 --> 00:02:05.658
Like, you've actually now
been recognized by people

00:02:05.658 --> 00:02:07.826
who have never seen
a single episode of "SNL."

00:02:07.826 --> 00:02:08.928
-Oh, 100%.

00:02:08.928 --> 00:02:10.362
Yeah, I mean, Thierry Henry

00:02:10.362 --> 00:02:12.565
and Tony Parker
being one of them.

00:02:12.565 --> 00:02:15.034
That was the thing early on.
You make the commercials,

00:02:15.034 --> 00:02:17.837
and, like, we'd joke about, you
know, "Go make a viral video."

00:02:17.837 --> 00:02:19.738
You don't do that.
Like, you make "Lazy Sunday,"

00:02:19.738 --> 00:02:21.674
and then it sets
the Internet on fire,

00:02:21.674 --> 00:02:23.443
and it introduces people
to YouTube.

00:02:23.443 --> 00:02:26.646
But, like -- and for the record,
I know I wasn't in that.

00:02:26.646 --> 00:02:29.215
I know you weren't in that,
but -- yeah, right.

00:02:29.215 --> 00:02:30.850
-I don't think it --
we were around

00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:32.051
when they came up with the idea,

00:02:32.051 --> 00:02:33.519
so I do think we deserve
a little bit of credit.

00:02:33.519 --> 00:02:35.288
-I feel like yeah, absolutely.

00:02:35.288 --> 00:02:37.023
And a little of this,
if Lorne would --

00:02:37.023 --> 00:02:39.893
-Just a little.
-Just a taste. Just a taste.

00:02:39.893 --> 00:02:42.562
But, yeah, me
and our buddy John Glazer

00:02:42.562 --> 00:02:45.131
were at the U.S. Open,
and I remember we were watching

00:02:45.131 --> 00:02:47.567
the tennis match there
in Queens, and I look down,

00:02:47.567 --> 00:02:50.403
and I see Thierry Henry
and Tony Parker,

00:02:50.403 --> 00:02:52.638
both of who I know
because they're legends.

00:02:52.638 --> 00:02:55.608
They like both look, and they're
like, "Hey," waving at me

00:02:55.608 --> 00:02:58.144
like they're 8-year-olds
waving at, you know,

00:02:58.144 --> 00:02:59.612
Ronald McDonald or something.

00:02:59.612 --> 00:03:01.714
And I'm like, why are they -- I
don't know what's going on here.

00:03:01.714 --> 00:03:04.250
Maybe they've seen "Hall Pass"
a bunch of times or something.

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:05.785
And then, after the match,
they come up,

00:03:05.785 --> 00:03:09.388
and they're just gushing
in their lovely French accents

00:03:09.388 --> 00:03:10.756
of how much -- "Ted Lasso.

00:03:10.756 --> 00:03:12.392
Oh, my gosh, Ted Lasso."

00:03:12.392 --> 00:03:14.360
And I was like,
"Wow, this thing --"

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:17.497
And then, Steve Nash was another
guy who I'm a huge fan of,

00:03:17.497 --> 00:03:19.165
and I think he's just,
you know, terrific.

00:03:19.165 --> 00:03:21.100
Huge soccer fan.
Big Tottenham supporter.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:24.670
And seeing him at a Jay-Z
concert out here in L.A.

00:03:24.670 --> 00:03:25.938
Like, he came up to me.

00:03:25.938 --> 00:03:27.907
I was like, this is --
this thing has got --

00:03:27.907 --> 00:03:32.712
This thing has got reach in ways
that I had no way to anticipate.

00:03:32.712 --> 00:03:36.015
-We did get to work with some
athletes in our days at "SNL."

00:03:36.015 --> 00:03:40.386
And I actually just went back
and watched the sketch you did

00:03:40.386 --> 00:03:43.656
with LeBron because I thought
the magic moment

00:03:43.656 --> 00:03:46.325
that I'm going to ask you about
had actually happened on-air

00:03:46.325 --> 00:03:48.328
and they cut away,
but it was dress or rehearsal.

00:03:48.328 --> 00:03:50.229
-It wasn't even dress.
It was rehearsal.

00:03:50.229 --> 00:03:52.598
It was, like, either Thursday
or Friday rehearsal, yeah.

00:03:52.598 --> 00:03:54.867
-But I want you to know
that I also remembered it.

00:03:54.867 --> 00:03:56.936
-You do? Okay.
-Yes.

00:03:56.936 --> 00:04:01.140
-That's good because, you know,
you feel like you're gonna go --

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:05.177
-Now, I might remember it
because you told everybody

00:04:05.177 --> 00:04:06.279
like multiple times
after it happened.

00:04:06.279 --> 00:04:10.015
-I tell it a lot.
I tell it a lot.

00:04:10.015 --> 00:04:11.384
That's what I've learned from
our president.

00:04:11.384 --> 00:04:13.118
You just say the same thing
over and over.

00:04:13.118 --> 00:04:15.888
Some segment of the population
will remember, but...

00:04:15.888 --> 00:04:17.690
-So share your LeBron story.

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:20.326
-Well, the whole sketch was
it was this thing that

00:04:20.326 --> 00:04:22.261
Bill Hader and I used to do
where I would play, like,

00:04:22.261 --> 00:04:24.096
a hothead sort of
like stagehand.

00:04:24.096 --> 00:04:25.564
Like, it was
a boom mike operator

00:04:25.564 --> 00:04:27.032
with Julia Louis-Dreyfus,
and with LeBron,

00:04:27.032 --> 00:04:28.534
I was like the guy
handing him his props,

00:04:28.534 --> 00:04:29.902
and he's doing a commercial.

00:04:29.902 --> 00:04:32.038
And I eventually get to a place
where I just challenge

00:04:32.038 --> 00:04:36.875
LeBron James as basically myself
to one-on-one, and he --

00:04:36.875 --> 00:04:38.477
you know, he scores on me
very easily.

00:04:38.477 --> 00:04:39.511
He hits me with an elbow.

00:04:39.511 --> 00:04:41.714
I pop up with blood
all over my face.

00:04:41.714 --> 00:04:44.384
And then, the way the sketch
ends, I think the term

00:04:44.384 --> 00:04:46.252
we would use at "SNL"
is "rave out,"

00:04:46.252 --> 00:04:48.754
where I would then get the ball
with blood all over my face

00:04:48.754 --> 00:04:51.457
and then try to take him
to the hole again as the crowd,

00:04:51.457 --> 00:04:53.058
you know, claps hopefully.

00:04:53.058 --> 00:04:55.060
And so, at rehearsal, you know,

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:56.863
you run through it
usually like three times.

00:04:56.863 --> 00:05:00.233
And the very first time
we did it, I went down,

00:05:00.233 --> 00:05:01.834
and the hoop was probably,
you know,

00:05:01.834 --> 00:05:03.102
8 1/2 feet
or something like that.

00:05:03.102 --> 00:05:04.370
I went down the left side.

00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:06.339
I went up, you know, like that,
and reversed it

00:05:06.339 --> 00:05:08.775
and came underneath
and kissed it off the glass

00:05:08.775 --> 00:05:10.542
with a little spin,
and it went in.

00:05:10.542 --> 00:05:13.779
And all the camera guys,
everybody on the set

00:05:13.779 --> 00:05:14.914
were like, "Whoa!"

00:05:14.914 --> 00:05:16.815
I'm pretty sure Maverick,
who, you know,

00:05:16.815 --> 00:05:19.185
is LeBron's, you know,
right-hand man, was there too.

00:05:19.185 --> 00:05:20.519
I was just like, "Oh, snap."

00:05:20.519 --> 00:05:22.855
And LeBron came over
and gave me a little love.

00:05:22.855 --> 00:05:24.089
And I was like, "Okay, great."

00:05:24.089 --> 00:05:27.893
And I beelined it
to the control room

00:05:27.893 --> 00:05:29.295
because we film
all those things,

00:05:29.295 --> 00:05:31.430
and as a writer and as an actor,
you get to watch the shots

00:05:31.430 --> 00:05:33.298
and see what works
and what doesn't.

00:05:33.298 --> 00:05:36.902
And I quickly got a DVD of it
burned and went to go watch it,

00:05:36.902 --> 00:05:39.071
and you see me go to the hole.

00:05:39.071 --> 00:05:42.575
It fades to black as
the sketches do in rehearsal,

00:05:42.575 --> 00:05:46.445
but you hear the audio of, like,
the crowd going "Ah!"

00:05:46.445 --> 00:05:48.014
Like that.

00:05:48.014 --> 00:05:50.349
And that's it. It only exists
through oral tradition.

00:05:50.349 --> 00:05:53.251
So thank you for allowing me
to spread this lie continuously.

00:05:53.251 --> 00:05:55.321
-Of course. Not a lie.

00:05:55.321 --> 00:05:57.690
-It really happened.
-In my head, I saw it happen.

00:05:57.690 --> 00:06:00.659
So maybe I was on the floor
for it, or maybe you just tell

00:06:00.659 --> 00:06:03.362
a story that has great
visual details to it.

00:06:03.362 --> 00:06:04.797
-Me and Garrison Keillor.

00:06:04.797 --> 00:06:07.633
[ Laughter ]

00:06:07.633 --> 00:06:09.435
-Hey, congrats on the show,
buddy.

00:06:09.435 --> 00:06:10.469
-Thank you.

00:06:10.469 --> 00:06:11.904
-It's always a delight
to see you.

00:06:11.904 --> 00:06:12.938
-Likewise.

00:06:12.938 --> 00:06:14.273
-Hopefully back in the city
soon.

00:06:14.273 --> 00:06:16.208
-Yeah. We'll get the kids
together and have them do

00:06:16.208 --> 00:06:19.011
some improv and, you know,
all that stuff. Right?

00:06:19.011 --> 00:06:21.146
From six feet distance,
of course.

00:06:21.146 --> 00:06:22.215
-Alright, be well, buddy.

